      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.624,
            0.261,
            0.744,
            0.306
          ],
          "content": "What do we get when this piece is moved?"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.585,
            0.046,
            0.862,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "We Distribute, Yet Things Multiply"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.211,
            0.106
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Hint:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.099,
            0.745,
            0.318
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.345,
            0.848,
            0.403
          ],
          "content": "Sridharacharya (750 CE) gave an interesting method to quickly compute the squares of numbers using Identity 1C! Consider the following modified form of this identity —"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.394,
            0.405,
            0.605,
            0.425
          ],
          "content": "a^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)+b^{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.441,
            0.403,
            0.464
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Why is this identity true?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.469,
            0.75,
            0.49
          ],
          "content": "Now, for example, $3 1 ^ { 2 }$ can be found by taking $a \\equiv 3 1$ and $b = 1$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.43,
            0.492,
            0.678,
            0.573
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}31^{2} &= (31 + 1)(31 - 1) + 1^{2} \\\\&= 32 \\times 30 + 1 \\\\&= 961.\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.576,
            0.621,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": "$1 9 7 ^ { 2 }$ can be found by taking $a = 1 9 7 ,$ and $b = 3$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.412,
            0.6,
            0.692,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}197^{2} &= (197 + 3) (197 - 3) + 3^{2} \\\\&= 200 \\times 194 + 9 \\\\&= 38809.\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.697,
            0.293,
            0.72
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.722,
            0.733,
            0.744
          ],
          "content": "1. Which is greater: $( a - b ) ^ { 2 }$ or $( b - a ) ^ { 2 } ?$ Justify your answer."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.752,
            0.625,
            0.773
          ],
          "content": "2. Express 100 as the difference of two squares."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.813,
            0.714,
            0.901,
            0.82
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.781,
            0.798,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": "3. Find 406<sup>2</sup>, 72<sup>2</sup>, 145<sup>2</sup>, $1 0 9 7 ^ { 2 } ,$ and $1 2 4 ^ { 2 }$ using the identities you have learnt so far."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.831,
            0.798,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": "4. Do Patterns 1 and 2 hold only for counting numbers? Do they hold for negative integers as well? What about fractions? Justify your answer."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
